WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.460
- [Voiceover] In early 2020,
Leigh Whannell raked in

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.890
over $100 million at the box office

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:09.030
on just a $7 million budget,
and breathed new life

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:12.270
into Universal's previously
comatose Dark Universe.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:13.103
- Go.

00:00:13.103 --> 00:00:13.977
- [Voiceover] And he did by...

00:00:13.977 --> 00:00:14.810
(woman gasps)

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:17.990
scaring the pants off of
us with Invisible Man,

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:19.400
which is an incredible feat,

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.260
considering the villain
is, well, invisible.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.960
How is it that lead
Elizabeth Moss could have us

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.740
peeking through our fingers with fear

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:29.640
from just gazing across an empty kitchen?

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.660
How did Stephen Spielberg
and John Williams

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.680
send chills down our collective spines

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.930
with just two notes on the piano?

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:39.340
And why does Hitchcock's swirl
of blood in black and white

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:42.050
still have us checking who
else is in the bathroom

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.023
every time we step into the shower?

00:00:44.023 --> 00:00:47.435
(water running)

00:00:47.435 --> 00:00:49.060
(woman gasps)

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:51.550
So let's break down what exactly scares us

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.890
at the movies today and how
that's evolved over time.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:56.600
The history of horror on screen

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:59.480
dates back to before the
Golden Age of Hollywood,

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.450
but our concept of a horror
movie really kicked off

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.590
with Universal's monster films.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.100
- It's alive!

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.100
- [Voiceover] Dracula,
Frankenstein, the Mummy,

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.130
and yes, the Invisible Man,
all released in the early 1930s

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:14.460
and gave birth not just
to some of the oldest

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.310
film franchises on screen,

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.460
but also gave us one of the defining ways

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.320
that filmmakers would employ to terrify us

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:23.860
for decades to come.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.566
In this case, monsters.

00:01:25.566 --> 00:01:26.700
(Frankenstein screams)

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.090
Any horror movie or horror
hybrid can be broken down

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.740
into one of these essential scare tactics:

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:37.370
monsters, suspense, gore,
the sudden or unexpected

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:41.120
and the idea that seemingly
ordinary aspects of our lives

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:42.976
are in fact terrifying.

00:01:42.976 --> 00:01:45.976
(demonic screaming)

00:02:01.578 --> 00:02:03.710
And although there's
overlap and exceptions,

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:06.130
these sub-genres also
mirror the progression

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:08.270
of horror films over the decades.

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:10.420
Universal Studios kicked things off

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:11.710
with their monster movies

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:14.540
in what would become known
as their Dark Universe.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.900
- I am Dracula.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.150
- [Voiceover] Although Dracula
is considered the kickoff

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.150
of the Universal monster
series, the studio's founder,

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.100
Carl Laemmle Jr really
set the mold earlier

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.550
when he produced The Phantom of the Opera.

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:31.620
This 1925 silent film adaption
of the Phantom of the Opera

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.420
also gave us our first
jump scare on screen,

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.202
but more on that later.

00:02:36.202 --> 00:02:38.310
(spooky music)

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:40.960
Where the early monster movies
just presented a villain

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.610
built to scare and didn't
focus on the bloody acts

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:46.580
they were committing, as horror
films became more prevalent,

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:49.560
Frankenstein's scarred
face would prove not enough

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.010
to get audiences on the
edge of their seats.

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:54.000
It was time to bring on the blood.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.440
Many early Hollywood films
leaned into horror and gore,

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.030
and their popularity
inadvertently derailed

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.550
that part of the genre for years.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:05.820
(crowd singing)

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.460
Tod Browning's Freaks
in 1932 was so horrific,

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:11.040
even after several edits,

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.490
it prompted Hollywood to enact
the Hays Production Code,

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:17.270
which cut down on the
sex, gore and violence

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:19.800
that we horror fans have
come to know and love.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.860
In response to the Hays
Codes, filmmakers had to rely

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.890
on innuendo, clever camera work, scoring

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:29.790
and the use of dread and
suspense to horrify film fans.

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:32.500
The artistry behind that became the norm

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:36.040
and no one did it better
than the master himself,

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.300
Alfred Hitchcock.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.103
- We all go a little mad sometimes.

00:03:42.553 --> 00:03:43.540
Haven't you?

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:46.270
- [Voiceover] Let's take a peek
at his infamous shower scene

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:49.850
from Psycho, a moment from
a movie that is so iconic,

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:52.340
it is the basis of an entire documentary

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:55.122
and has been imitated countless times.

00:03:55.122 --> 00:03:56.812
(shower running)

00:03:56.812 --> 00:03:57.645
(woman screams)

00:03:57.645 --> 00:04:00.440
A moment of violence with
very little blood or gore

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:03.100
that suggests an idea so unnerving,

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.490
movies themselves would never be the same.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:09.100
(woman screaming)

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.510
Hitchcock was dubbed
the master of suspense,

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:14.130
and if you just have a
listen to his Bomb Theory,

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:15.629
you'll understand why.

00:04:15.629 --> 00:04:18.800
- Four people are sitting around a table

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.280
talking about baseball, whatever you like.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:25.280
Suddenly a bomb goes off.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.200
What did the audience have?

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.250
10 seconds of shock.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:32.140
Now take the same scene
and tell the audience

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:35.362
there was a bomb under that table,

00:04:35.362 --> 00:04:37.463
and will go off in five minutes.

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:42.060
Well, the whole emotion of the
audience is totally different

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.273
because you've given
them that information.

00:04:44.273 --> 00:04:45.630
- [Voiceover] That type of suspense

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:48.670
is what John Krasinski
used in A Quiet Place.

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:51.660
The opening sequence is
essentially a cinematic way

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:54.700
of communicating that there's
a bomb in the floor boards

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:56.234
without saying anything.

00:04:56.234 --> 00:04:57.067
(toy trilling)

00:04:57.067 --> 00:04:59.450
We see the family scared and silent,

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:01.870
we know there's a reason
why they can't talk

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:05.820
and then the spaceship
becomes the ticking bomb.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.525
Oh, that poor kid.

00:05:07.525 --> 00:05:09.310
(creature screeches)

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:12.840
For 30 years, the Hays Code
of straight edge filmmaking

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.710
was the law of the land,
and during that time,

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:18.540
filmmakers like Hitchcock,
Stanley Kubrick,

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.500
Billy Wilder and others
used creative methods

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:24.780
to incite fear while keeping things PG.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:26.280
- Open the pod bay doors, Hal.

00:05:27.893 --> 00:05:31.463
- [Hal] I'm sorry Dave,
I'm afraid I can't do that.

00:05:31.463 --> 00:05:34.000
- [Voiceover] I mean,
look at 1944's Gaslight,

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.610
which gave Ingrid Bergman
her first Oscar win

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:38.780
and gave the Internet some new slang.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:40.700
It's the dread the audience feels

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.240
will happen to our heroine that hooks us.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:47.090
Bergman gives great face as
she despairs and questions

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:48.930
if what she's seeing is real.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.577
- I can see you still
standing there and saying,

00:05:52.577 --> 00:05:57.445
"Look, look at this letter",
and staring at nothing.

00:05:57.445 --> 00:05:58.278
- What?

00:05:58.278 --> 00:05:59.840
- You had nothing in your hand!

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:01.550
- [Voiceover] This is the
reverse of what happens

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:03.930
to Elizabeth Moss in The Invisible Man,

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.240
but the dread created is the same.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:09.060
In the 2020 film, we don't feel dread

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:10.770
over whether she'll figure it out,

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:13.960
it's more the dread that
no one will believe her

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:16.370
and the other horrors inflicted upon her.

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:17.510
- [Cee] James, it was him!

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:18.343
He's here, I swear to you.

00:06:18.343 --> 00:06:19.980
- Enough, enough!

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:21.620
Cee, enough.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:24.160
- [Voiceover] This is the same
reason why we fall in love

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.330
with the final girls that nobody believes,

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:29.860
like Laurie Strode, Ellen
Ripley and Sidney Prescott.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:32.090
Well, at least we do
if they don't have sex,

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:33.660
but more on that later.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:36.710
Besides using dread and suspense
to ratchet up the scares,

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:40.090
Gaslight also employed one of
the other tried and true ways

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:44.000
to scare the audience, by
making the spouse the villain.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.040
Suddenly, nothing is safe,

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:49.690
not your partner, not even the
things and places around you.

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:51.500
In the hands of a horror master,

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:53.640
something innocuous in normal life

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.630
becomes sinister on screen,

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:59.570
like birds or the sound of
wind, spiders or heights,

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:03.003
or even your childhood doll or pet.

00:07:03.003 --> 00:07:04.560
(woman gasping)

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.640
Several horror masters reveled in the idea

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:09.460
that life is more disturbing

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:12.100
than the most evil movie monster.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:14.320
Sometimes, that meant that
you should fear birds.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:15.810
Sometimes, that meant that you should fear

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:18.290
the system we live in,
or how we live in it.

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:20.620
Jordan Peele used the horrors of racism

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:22.700
and the marginalization of black Americans

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:24.200
to create the sunken place.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:29.200
- So the movie, for me, became
almost about representation

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:32.750
within the genre, within
itself, in a weird way.

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:34.940
- [Voiceover] Ari Aster
used the cult sub-genre

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:38.090
to turn the idea of a fun
summer vacation on its head

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:41.030
and to explore grief
and toxic masculinity,

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:45.513
all while featuring a ton of
gore as, well, just a bonus.

00:07:47.003 --> 00:07:49.336
(man falls)

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:52.110
And just earlier this year,

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:54.660
Natalie Erika James framed Alzheimer's

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:56.690
as a gruesome horror plot point

00:07:56.690 --> 00:07:59.360
for the equally terrifying Relic.

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:03.673
Camera, scoring, lighting.

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:06.453
- Nice boy.

00:08:06.453 --> 00:08:08.890
- [Voiceover] Dread and
the presence of monsters

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:10.787
all work to ignite fear.

00:08:10.787 --> 00:08:13.620
(woman screaming)

00:08:14.541 --> 00:08:15.900
(horror music)

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:19.300
But it can't be overstated
just how much can be achieved

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:22.829
with the right application of
red corn syrup over pigskin.

00:08:22.829 --> 00:08:25.496
(door creaking)

00:08:32.149 --> 00:08:32.982
(creature shrieks)

00:08:32.982 --> 00:08:36.830
In the 2000s, films like
Hostel, Audition and Saw

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:39.930
reminded audiences what
the Texas Chainsaw Massacre

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:42.680
and slasher films knew all too well.

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:46.669
Blood and gore can terrify
people to their core.

00:08:46.669 --> 00:08:49.169
(man running)

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:52.800
(bones breaking)

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:55.000
John Carpenter, Dario Argento,

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.630
Roger Corman and George
Romero gave birth to a new era

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:02.060
in horror filmmaking and
solidified the horror tropes

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:04.830
that are now as familiar as A-list stars.

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:06.900
And speaking of familiar,
we couldn't go any further

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:09.940
without (creature shrieks)
mentioning the horror tactic

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:11.450
that gets the most flak,

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:14.400
but remains, to this
day, the most effective:

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:15.233
the jump scare.

00:09:17.381 --> 00:09:18.214
(person claps)

00:09:18.214 --> 00:09:20.550
The shock of the unexpected crashing noise

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:24.833
or leaping villain is a quick
and cheap way to get screams.

00:09:24.833 --> 00:09:25.666
(man gasps)

00:09:25.666 --> 00:09:28.900
If a monster or ghost story
doesn't have at least a handful,

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:30.500
it's shocking.

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:31.490
(horror music)

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:34.080
Using camera work and
sound, a director can,

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.820
in just a few quick moments,

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:38.180
change the energy around any moment.

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:40.840
(phone ringing)

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:42.840
And that shock is not just reserved

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:45.020
for monsters jumping out of a closet.

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:46.830
Look at the ending of the first Saw film

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:50.860
when Jigsaw gets up after Cary
Elwes has cut off his foot.

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:52.260
The shock in that moment

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:55.750
rivals just about everything
that happened up to that point

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:57.700
as far as how much it affected us.

00:09:57.700 --> 00:09:59.089
- Game over.

00:09:59.089 --> 00:10:00.070
(person screaming)

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:01.280
- [Voiceover] The same can be said

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:03.270
for Robert Eggers' The Witch.

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:06.150
Though primarily an
atmospheric horror film,

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:09.630
Eggers splices quick cuts
of disturbing pagan imagery

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:12.990
into the nearly silent
film as just another way

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:15.250
to keep the audience ill at ease.

00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:17.450
But every aspect of a horror film

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:20.060
becomes a trope after a
certain amount of time.

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:22.840
The virginal heroine, sin equals death.

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:25.790
- There are certain rules
that one must abide by

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:28.200
in order to successfully
survive a horror movie.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:30.460
- [Voiceover] Yes, basically
all the rules from Scream

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:34.580
took hold in the 1970s and
audiences could not get enough

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:37.610
of the midnight madness
double feature horror films

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:39.850
that filmmakers like Quentin Tarantino

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:43.560
would go on to celebrate
and recreate, or rip off,

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:45.310
depending on who you ask.

00:10:45.310 --> 00:10:47.350
But by the time you reach the 1990s

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:50.640
and Jamie Kennedy forever ruined
most of the horror staples

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:53.070
filmmakers had relied on for so long,

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:56.190
the latest and most effective
mode to terrorize us

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:57.627
rose to prominence.

00:10:57.627 --> 00:10:58.820
- Get out!

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:00.680
- Yo, yo chill man.

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:01.770
- Get out!

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:05.300
- [Voiceover] And that was the
subversion of expectations,

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:08.690
which is the entire premise
of Cabin in the Woods.

00:11:08.690 --> 00:11:11.930
Spoiler alert, the film
lives in the standard

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:14.670
teen horror model until the big reveal,

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:17.720
in which we find out the
events up until that point

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:20.800
are, in fact, a tribute
to the Ancient Ones,

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.380
a cruel group of subterranean deities

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:26.660
who are trying to determine
if humanity is worth saving.

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:29.190
- I'm sorry I let you get
attacked by a werewolf

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:30.633
and then ended the world.

00:11:32.436 --> 00:11:36.233
- No, you were right.

00:11:37.646 --> 00:11:38.913
Humanity.

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:42.120
- [Voiceover] And based on
the current state of events,

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:45.293
I have to agree with the final
scene and say, probably not.

00:11:48.530 --> 00:11:51.180
And in the end, the
subversion of expectations

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:53.960
is where we're scaring
people most these days.

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:56.440
As movie audiences became
more sophisticated,

00:11:56.440 --> 00:11:58.230
horror directors had to work harder

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:00.930
to get the desired effect,
making the ordinary

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:04.260
seem extraordinarily terrifying or scary.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:05.360
- Don't open the door.

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:10.310
- [Girl] See?

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:11.523
Everything's okay.

00:12:13.806 --> 00:12:15.750
(girl screams)

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:17.270
- [Voiceover] Jordan Peele used the image

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:20.570
of our worst selves
unleashed upon us in Us,

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:22.400
a thousand times scarier

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:24.800
than if it was just another
Freddie-styled villain

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:26.100
slicing up the town.

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:28.743
- Don't burn our house down.

00:12:31.425 --> 00:12:34.270
(demonic laughing)

00:12:34.270 --> 00:12:37.160
- [Voiceover] And Julia Ducournau
gave vegans and carnivores

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:39.440
something to chew on
with her cannibalistic

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:42.600
gory coming of age tale, Raw.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:45.230
Subverting our expectations
is the hallmark

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:48.513
of most successful modern
day horror directors.

00:12:57.278 --> 00:13:00.200
They call on the ancestors
and borrow just enough

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:02.780
to set the stage, and
then pull away the rules

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:04.900
that they have lived
by to keep us guessing,

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:08.063
and screaming, at the movie
magic they've created.

00:13:11.915 --> 00:13:12.748
(demon screaming)

00:13:12.748 --> 00:13:13.581
(woman screaming)

00:13:13.581 --> 00:13:16.510
And what was this year's
most successful horror film?

00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:17.890
The Invisible Man.

00:13:17.890 --> 00:13:20.840
Whannell's masterful
in setting expectations

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:22.540
and then making them disappear.

00:13:22.540 --> 00:13:24.980
He crafts a symphony of scares

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:27.663
by employing every scare
tactic we've mentioned.

00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:32.410
Jump scares are set up but never
happen, leaving us on edge.

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:34.800
Good people die, bad people live.

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:37.900
The movie's an old-school
bloodless suspense thriller

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:40.930
until we swim in pools of the stuff.

00:13:40.930 --> 00:13:43.530
And crucially, in the
biggest twist of the tale,

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:47.040
the focus is not on
the man but on a woman.

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:49.750
The terror, almost a
century after Universal

00:13:49.750 --> 00:13:52.970
started the genre, is not
what is becoming of him,

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:55.150
but what he's doing to her.

00:13:55.150 --> 00:13:57.630
By flipping the script,
Whannell found a fresh way

00:13:57.630 --> 00:14:00.990
to frighten us and a path
forward for horror filmmakers,

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:03.590
something horror fans who
thought they knew the genre

00:14:03.590 --> 00:14:06.273
inside and out, well, never saw coming.

